Licences in this repository
===========================

This repository holds a public report, the text we derive from it, and the code that builds that text. They have three different statuses. This file is a statement of those statuses, not legal advice.

1. The report's text
--------------------

The 9/11 Commission Report (22 July 2004) was published by the National Commission on Terrorist Attacks Upon the United States, a federal commission created by Act of Congress, and printed by the Government Publishing Office. The text carries no copyright notice. We treat it as a work of the United States Government, in the public domain. We have not found a statement of its status in terms, and commission members were not all federal employees.

The photographs in the report carry third-party credits (for example Reuters, New York Post); we serve text only, and any such material is not covered by this statement.

The files in reference/wikisource/ are the Wikisource volunteers' transcription, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0. They stay under that licence, are used only to measure our text and are not served.

2. Our markup, processing and editorial additions
-------------------------------------------------

The structure and Markdown formatting of full.md, paragraph and section identifiers, corrections.yaml, golden.yaml, baseline.json, fidelity.md, PROCESSING.md, datapackage.json, the README and any other words we wrote are licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International licence (CC BY 4.0, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode). Attribute them to "Reports that Matter (https://reportsthatmatter.org)". This does not change the status of the report's own text, described in section 1, which we do not own.

3. Code
-------

The code that builds the text (ingest.ts and other TypeScript, JavaScript and Python files, and package.json) is licensed under the MIT licence; see LICENSE-MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Rufus Pollock and contributors.

Other files
-----------

Files under archive/ are copies of the report as its publisher issued it, kept so the text can be rebuilt and checked; they have the status described in section 1.

Files under reference/ are copies of other editions of the report, kept to measure our text. They keep the licence recorded for them (in reference/manifest.json where one exists, or section 1 where this file says so) and are not covered by sections 2 and 3.
